A.update({'title':'HealthRX vs AgelessRx: price architecture, commitments, and fulfillment evidence','h1':'HealthRX vs AgelessRx: compare the bill, cadence, and evidence gaps','description':'A source-bound comparison of how HealthRX and AgelessRx describe price, commitment, review, pharmacy language, and delivery.','word_count':1600})
A['sections']=[{'heading':'Start with the rate conditions, not a subtraction','paragraphs':['HealthRX states a $133 monthly starting point and separately references $99 per month on a 12-month plan. AgelessRx states $159 per month. The lowest displayed HealthRX number therefore carries a duration condition; comparing $99 with $159 as if both were month-to-month quotes would be misleading.','Ask for the first charge, every later charge, minimum duration, dose-dependent changes, included medication, clinician fees, supplies, laboratory costs, and shipping. A starting rate can be accurate without representing the all-in invoice.','Neither figure determines medical fit. Both pages concern prescription pathways, and compounded products are not FDA-approved.'],},{'heading':'Commitment language answers a separate question','paragraphs':['AgelessRx states there are no long-term contracts and no cancellation fees. HealthRX states cancellation is available in one click while also displaying a lower 12-month rate. Those statements can coexist because cancellation mechanics and the conditions attached to a promotional rate are different fields.','Before paying, request the cancellation deadline, treatment of prepaid amounts, renewal date, refund treatment after pharmacy processing, and whether a lower rate changes if the plan ends early. Save the dated answer.','A flexible cancellation phrase does not freeze future pricing or guarantee uninterrupted medication supply.']},{'heading':'HealthRX publishes a more segmented review path','paragraphs':['The archived HealthRX page says an independent U.S. board-certified physician reviews the file within 24 hours and prescribes only if clinically appropriate. That describes a review stage; it does not promise approval.','The same page states free overnight shipping. Review time and shipping speed remain separate clocks, with pharmacy processing and prescription routing between them.','AgelessRx states licensed U.S. clinicians and unlimited messaging for dose questions and side effects. These are process facts, not comparative clinical-quality evidence.']},{'heading':'AgelessRx states a 3–5-business-day shipping window','paragraphs':['AgelessRx states free discreet shipping and a 3–5-business-day shipping period. The extract does not authorize a guarantee that an intake form becomes a doorstep delivery within that period.','Ask which event starts the window, which pharmacy dispenses, whether weekends count, how tracking is issued, and what happens after a damaged or delayed package.','HealthRX’s overnight statement and AgelessRx’s 3–5-day statement may begin at different operational milestones. The comparison preserves the language instead of manufacturing one common order-to-door metric.']},{'heading':'Payment language is not coverage','paragraphs':['HealthRX says no insurance is needed and HSA/FSA funds are eligible. AgelessRx says it accepts major cards plus HSA and FSA cards. Neither statement guarantees reimbursement under a particular plan.','Ask for itemized receipts and confirm plan rules before treating HSA/FSA acceptance as a net-cost reduction. Insurance coverage, account eligibility, and merchant acceptance are separate questions.','Compensation and affiliate readiness remain outside the evidence score. No outbound route is active in this held build.']},{'heading':'Product status stays next to the commercial facts','paragraphs':['Both archived pages describe compounded pathways. FDA states compounded drugs are not FDA-approved and are not reviewed before marketing for safety, effectiveness, or quality.','A licensed clinician or state-licensed pharmacy does not convert a compounded product into an FDA-approved drug. Verify the exact formulation and dispensing pharmacy before payment.','This comparison does not recommend a drug, dose, pharmacy, or provider.']},{'heading':'Use the pair as a verification worksheet','paragraphs':['HealthRX currently exposes more of the physician-review and shipping sequence. AgelessRx exposes a simple $159 start, no-long-term-contract language, and a 3–5-day shipping statement. Each advantage is limited to the archived evidence.','Build a written table for first charge, recurring charge, duration, clinician review, pharmacy, shipment start event, cancellation cutoff, and unresolved fields.','Recheck the first-party pages because price, availability, pharmacy relationships, state access, and policies can change.']}]
